dialogue is: {{ Doctor: Hi, welcome to the clinic. My name is Dr. Smith and I'm a GP. Can you confirm your name and date of birth for me, please?
Patient: Hi. Um, my name is Brittany Edwards, and my date of birth is September 14th, 1989.
Doctor: OK, thank you. Can you confirm your address for me, please?
Patient: I live at 1234 Main Street, Anytown, USA.
Doctor: OK, thank you. Um, can you tell me a bit about your symptoms, please?
Patient: OK. So, um, I've been having a lot of stomach pain and nausea and vomiting. I've been having these episodes, um, about every other week. Um, and I've been taking Bentyl for the past few months, and I've been taking it four times a day. But I still have these episodes.
Doctor: OK. And can you tell me more about the pain you've been having? Is it just in your tummy, or is it anywhere else?
Patient: Um, it's just in my tummy. It's usually in the lower part of my stomach. It's like a sharp pain. It's not really a dull ache. It's like a sharp pain. And it's usually accompanied by nausea and vomiting. And sometimes I get diarrhea.
Doctor: OK. And how often do you have diarrhea?
Patient: Um, I would say, um, usually two to three times a day. It's not always diarrhea, it's just like loose stools.
Doctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any blood in your stool at all?
Patient: No, I don't have any blood in my stool.
Doctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any mucus in your stool at all?
Patient: No, I don't have any mucus in my stool.
Doctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any abdominal cramps?
Patient: Yes, I do have abdominal cramps. It's like, um, it's like my stomach is just cramping up and it's really uncomfortable.
Doctor: OK. And how long have you had these symptoms for?
Patient: Um, I've had them for about three months now.
Doctor: OK. And have you noticed any triggers for your symptoms?
Patient: Um, I think it's usually when I eat greasy foods, like fried foods or foods that are high in fat. And also, um, when I drink too much, I think that triggers it too.
Doctor: OK. And do you drink a lot of soda?
Patient: Um, I used to drink a lot of soda, but I've been trying to cut back on it. I've been drinking more water now.
Doctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any stress in your life that you're not managing well?
Patient: Yeah, I have a lot of stress. I have a lot of anxiety. I work as a nurse and I have a lot of stress at work. And also, um, I have a lot of stress at home. My husband and I have a lot of disagreements.
Doctor: OK. And how do you usually manage your stress?
Patient: Um, I try to take deep breaths and I try to meditate. I also try to exercise. I like to go for walks.
Doctor: OK. And do you have any other symptoms that you've noticed? Any other symptoms that you've noticed in your body, like, uh, any joint pain, any muscle pain?
Patient: No, I don't have any joint pain or muscle pain.
Doctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any headaches?
Patient: Um, I do have headaches. But I don't know if it's related to my stomach pain or not.
Doctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any fever?
Patient: No, I don't have a fever.
Doctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any chills?
Patient: No, I don't have chills.
Doctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any vomiting?
Patient: Yes, I do have vomiting. But I don't know if it's related to my stomach pain or not.
Doctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any diarrhea?
Patient: Yes, I do have diarrhea.
Doctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any abdominal pain?
Patient: Yes, I do have abdominal pain.
Doctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any constipation?
Patient: No, I don't have constipation.
Doctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any difficulty with your bowels?
Patient: Um, I do have difficulty with my bowels. I have trouble going to the bathroom. It's like my bowels are constipated.
Doctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any weight loss?
Patient: No, I haven't had any weight loss.
Doctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any night sweats?
Patient: No, I haven't had any night sweats.
Doctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any hot flashes?
Patient: No, I haven't had any hot flashes.
Doctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any vaginal bleeding?
Patient: No, I haven't had any vaginal bleeding.
Doctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any vaginal discharge?
Patient: No, I haven't had any vaginal discharge.
Doctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal masses?
Patient: No, I don't have any abdominal masses.
Doctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal tenderness?
Patient: Yes, I do have abdominal tenderness.
Doctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal distension?
Patient: Yes, I do have abdominal distension.
Doctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal guarding?
Patient: Yes, I do have abdominal guarding.
Doctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal rebound tenderness?
Patient: Yes, I do have abdominal rebound tenderness.
Doctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal masses that you can feel?
Patient: No, I don't have any abdominal masses that I can feel.
Doctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal tenderness that you can feel?
Patient: Yes, I do have abdominal tenderness that I can feel.
Doctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal distension that you can feel?
Patient: Yes, I do have abdominal distension that I can feel.
Doctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal guarding that you can feel?
Patient: Yes, I do have abdominal guarding that I can feel.
