dialogue is: {{ Doctor: Hi there, welcome to the clinic. My name is Doctor Smith. How are you doing today?

Patient: Hi, I'm fine. Thank you.

Doctor: Good. Can you tell me a bit more about what's been going on with your back?

Patient: Yeah, so I've been having some lower back pain, and it started a few months ago. And I've been trying to treat it with ibuprofen and heat and ice, but it's not really been helping.

Doctor: Okay. And when did the pain start?

Patient: Um, I think it started about four months ago. I was moving some boxes out of my son's college dorm and I twisted and turned and felt a sharp pain in my lower back.

Doctor: Okay. And how has the pain been since then? Has it been constant or has it come and gone?

Patient: It's been pretty consistent. It's been pretty much every day. Sometimes it's a dull ache, sometimes it's a sharp pain. And sometimes it's more sore when I move certain ways.

Doctor: Okay. And what kind of movements make it worse?

Patient: Um, rolling over in my sleep, twisting, bending. Any kind of movement that involves twisting or bending really makes it worse.

Doctor: Okay. And how would you rate the pain on a scale of one to ten, one being very mild and ten being very severe?

Patient: I would say it's probably around a seven.

Doctor: Okay. And have you noticed any numbness or tingling in your legs?

Patient: Yeah, when I first started having the pain, I had tingling in my toes, but that went away after a few days.

Doctor: Okay. And have you noticed any weakness in your legs at all?

